Part 1: Theoretical Understanding (40%)
Q1: Explain the primary differences between TensorFlow and PyTorch. When would you choose one over the other?
Differences
Feature	TensorFlow	PyTorch
Computation Graph	Uses static graphs (Graph mode), but also supports eager execution	Uses dynamic computation graphs (eager by default)
Ease of Debugging	Harder (older versions); debugging graphs can be complex	Very easy—feels like normal Python, excellent debugging
Deployment	TensorFlow Serving, TensorFlow Lite, TensorFlow.js → very strong production tools	Deployment improving but still not as integrated as TensorFlow
Community	Strong in industry and large companies	Very strong in research and universities
When to choose which

Choose TensorFlow when:

Deploying to production (mobile, web, cloud)

You need TensorFlow Lite, TF.js, or Google Cloud integration

You want a full ecosystem (Keras, TF-serving, TPU support)

Choose PyTorch when:

Doing research or exploring new deep learning architectures

You want fast prototyping and easier debugging

You want code that feels natural and Pythonic

Q2: Describe two use cases for Jupyter Notebooks in AI development.
1. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Jupyter allows you to:

load datasets

clean data

visualize results with plots

test small code chunks interactively

Perfect for understanding data before training models.

2. Building and Demonstrating Machine Learning Experiments

You can:

train models step-by-step

view metrics immediately

record results in the same document

combine text, code, and charts

This makes notebooks ideal for tutorials, research, and presentations.

Q3: How does spaCy enhance NLP tasks compared to basic Python string operations?
1. Advanced Linguistic Processing

spaCy can perform:

tokenization

part-of-speech tagging

named entity recognition

dependency parsing

These are impossible or very hard using plain string functions.

2. Speed and Accuracy

spaCy is:

optimized in Cython → extremely fast

provides high-accuracy pretrained models

consistent across large text datasets

Basic string operations only split by characters and cannot understand grammar, meaning, or context.

✅ Part 2: Comparative Analysis
Compare Scikit-learn and TensorFlow
1. Target Applications
Library	Best For
Scikit-learn	Classical ML: decision trees, SVMs, clustering, regression, PCA, feature engineering
TensorFlow	Deep learning: neural networks, CNNs, RNNs, transformers

Scikit-learn cannot train deep networks; TensorFlow is built for them.

2. Ease of Use for Beginners

Scikit-learn

Very simple API

Works with small datasets

Fit → Predict workflow is easy

Best for students starting ML

TensorFlow

More complex

Requires understanding tensors, gradients, training loops

Keras makes it easier but still heavier than scikit-learn

Winner for beginners: Scikit-learn

3. Community Support

Both have very strong communities, but:

TensorFlow has huge industry support (Google, cloud deployment)

PyTorch dominates research

Scikit-learn is the standard for traditional ML

In terms of maturity for classical ML, Scikit-learn wins.
For deep learning and deployment, TensorFlow wins.
